    "title": "pandas.Categorical.categories — pandas 3.1.0.dev0 documentation",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# pandas.Categorical.categories#\n\n- property Categorical.categories[source]#\n- The categories of this categorical. Setting assigns new values to each category (effectively a rename of each individual category). The assigned value has to be a list-like object. All items must be unique and the number of items in the new categories must be the same as the number of items in the old categories. \n  - Raises:\n    - ValueError\n    - If the new categories do not validate as categories or if the number of new categories is unequal the number of old categories\n See also \n  - `rename_categories`\n  - Rename categories.\n  - `reorder_categories`\n  - Reorder categories.\n  - `add_categories`\n  - Add new categories.\n  - `remove_categories`\n  - Remove the specified categories.\n  - `remove_unused_categories`\n  - Remove categories which are not used.\n  - `set_categories`\n  - Set the categories to the specified ones.\n Examples For `pandas.Series` :>>> ser = pd.Series([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"a\"], dtype=\"category\") >>> ser.cat.categories Index(['a', 'b', 'c'], dtype='str') >>> raw_cat = pd.Categorical([None, \"b\", \"c\", None], categories=[\"b\", \"c\", \"d\"]) >>> ser = pd.Series(raw_cat) >>> ser.cat.categories Index(['b', 'c', 'd'], dtype='str') For `pandas.Categorical` :>>> cat = pd.Categorical([\"a\", \"b\"], ordered=True) >>> cat.categories Index(['a', 'b'], dtype='str') For `pandas.CategoricalIndex` :>>> ci = pd.CategoricalIndex([\"a\", \"c\", \"b\", \"a\", \"c\", \"b\"]) >>> ci.categories Index(['a', 'b', 'c'], dtype='str') >>> ci = pd.CategoricalIndex([\"a\", \"c\"], categories=[\"c\", \"b\", \"a\"]) >>> ci.categories Index(['c', 'b', 'a'], dtype='str')",
    "markdown_length": 1624,
